README-EDITORS -- seven editors that are not vi, and which to takeDOC/README-VI covers the two vi editors.  This file is the rest: fiveEmacsen, a line editor and a binary editor.Take `beav' to edit binary files.  For Emacs keys take `mg' -- or `umacs'if 45K matters, or `emacs' if you want the macro library.  For ordinarytext, take a vi.THE SEVEN---------  file      bytes  cio  module  source              docs  ----------------------------------------------------------------  em        79590  no   em      --                  DOC/em  me       110408  no   me      --                  DOC/em  emacs    144910  yes  emacs   SRC/memacs400_src   DOC/emacs  umacs     45412  yes  uMacs   --                  DOC/em  mg        79126  yes  mg      SRC/mg2a            DOC/mg  ed        85810  yes  ed      SRC/ed              DOC/ed  beav      85950  no   beav    SRC/beav            DOC/beav`cio' is Microware's module.  It ships here, so on this disk it costsnothing; it is one more file to carry if you take the editor away.DOC/README-CIO.FIVE EMACSEN, FOUR OF THEM ONE PROGRAM--------------------------------------Four are MicroEMACS, at four versions and two sizes:    em          3.8b      the oldest here    me          3.11      German messages in places    emacs       4.00      the newest and the biggest; reads a macro library    umacs       1.0       uMacs, a cut-down MicroEMACS in a third the spaceThe fifth, `mg', is MicroGnuEmacs: its key bindings follow GNU Emacs, andit has no macro language.  If you know GNU Emacs, take `mg'; if you knowMicroEMACS, any of the other four will feel right.  You want GNU Emacs bindings                         mg  You want the macro library and the newest    MicroEMACS, and can carry 145K + USR/LIB/EMACS    emacs  You want the smallest thing with Emacs keys         umacs, 45K`em' and `me' do nothing the three above do not.What each wants besides itself:    emacs   .emacsrc, emacs.hlp and its macros, looked for in your home            directory, the current one and each PATH directory, then in            memacs400 on /h0 or /h1.  The macros ship in USR/LIB/EMACS            and emacs.hlp in SYS: put those two on PATH and ESC ? finds            the help.    umacs   SYS/umacs.hlp.  Your own OS-9 may have a `umacs' too;            DOC/README-NAMES says which one answers.    mg      nothing; it reads /dd/sys/mgrc if you give it one    ed      a RAM disk at /r0 for its scratch file: `mount -r=256k /r0'TAKE THIS IF------------  beav    you edit binary.  It edits a file as bytes on a full screen, and          its source is here.  `hexed' is a different idea: it dumps to a          text editor and back.  Type hex digits to change the byte under          the cursor; Ctl-C then y leaves without saving.  mg      you want Emacs keys in 79K, with no macro language.  emacs   you want the macros.  umacs   you want Emacs keys in 45K.  Its module is called `uMacs'.  ed      you are writing a script that edits a file without a screen.  em, me  the above do not suit.All the Emacsen quit with Ctl-X Ctl-C.